---
{
  title: "top_n"
}
---
### NAME

    top_n() - The elements with the highest scores, highest first. By
    default ties share a place, so everyone within the top n distinct scores
    is kept: with scores 15, 15, 14, 13 and n of 3, all four are. Strict
    keeps exactly n elements, breaking ties at the cut arbitrarily.

### SYNOPSIS

    varargs mixed *top_n( mixed *arr, int n, mixed fn, int strict );

### DESCRIPTION

    The elements with the highest scores, highest first. By default ties
    share a place, so everyone within the top n distinct scores is kept:
    with scores 15, 15, 14, 13 and n of 3, all four are. Strict keeps
    exactly n elements, breaking ties at the cut arbitrarily.

    Without a function, each element is its own score.

### PARAMETERS

    arr     (mixed*)
            The elements to rank.

    n       (int)
            How many places to keep.

    fn      (function|int, optional)
            Returns each element's score; or strict, when an int.

    strict  (int, optional, default: 0)
            Whether to keep exactly n elements, when fn is a function.

### RETURN VALUES

    (mixed*) The kept elements, highest score first.

### ERRORS

    If arr is not an array If n is not a non-negative integer If fn is not
    undefined, an integer or a function

### EXAMPLE

    {{di1}}top_n(({ 15, 13, 15, 14 }), 3);    // ({ 15, 15, 14, 13 })
    top_n(({ 15, 13, 15, 14 }), 3, 1); // ({ 15, 15, 14 })

    mapping visits = ([ "Ana" : 15, "Bo" : 15, "Cy" : 14, "Di" : 13 ]);
    top_n(keys(visits), 1, (: $(visits)[$1] :)); // ({ "Ana", "Bo" }){{di0}}
